DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     Counting semaphores provide a mechanism for synchronizing access to a
     pool of resources.  Unlike mutexes, semaphores do not have the concept of
     an owner, so they can also be useful in situations where one thread needs
     to acquire a resource, and another thread needs to release it.  Each sem‐
     aphore has an integer value associated with it.  Posting (incrementing)
     always succeeds, but waiting (decrementing) can only successfully com‐
     plete if the resulting value of the semaphore is greater than or equal to
     zero.

     Semaphores should not be used where mutexes and condition variables will
     suffice.  Semaphores are a more complex synchronization mechanism than
     mutexes and condition variables, and are not as efficient.

     Semaphores are created with sseemmaa__iinniitt(), where _s_e_m_a is a pointer to space
     for a _s_t_r_u_c_t _s_e_m_a, _v_a_l_u_e is the initial value of the semaphore, and
     _d_e_s_c_r_i_p_t_i_o_n is a pointer to a null-terminated character string that de‐
     scribes the semaphore.  Semaphores are destroyed with sseemmaa__ddeessttrrooyy().  A
     semaphore is posted (incremented) with sseemmaa__ppoosstt().  A semaphore is
     waited on (decremented) with sseemmaa__wwaaiitt(), sseemmaa__ttiimmeeddwwaaiitt(), or
     sseemmaa__ttrryywwaaiitt().  The _t_i_m_o argument to sseemmaa__ttiimmeeddwwaaiitt() specifies the min‐
     imum time in ticks to wait before returning with failure.  sseemmaa__vvaalluuee()
     is used to read the current value of the semaphore.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEESS
     The sseemmaa__vvaalluuee() function returns the current value of the semaphore.

     If decrementing the semaphore would result in its value being negative,
     sseemmaa__ttrryywwaaiitt() returns 0 to indicate failure.  Otherwise, a non-zero
     value is returned to indicate success.

     The sseemmaa__ttiimmeeddwwaaiitt() function returns 0 if waiting on the semaphore suc‐
     ceeded; otherwise a non-zero error code is returned.

EERRRROORRSS
     The sseemmaa__ttiimmeeddwwaaiitt() function will fail if:

     [EWOULDBLOCK]      Timeout expired.

